To modify your robot so it can turn both ways when it detects white, allowing it to continue in a more efficient manner without doing a full turn, you can adjust the logic in your code. Here’s how you can approach this:

1. **Reference to Course Material**: This concept is related to the "Line Follower" section, particularly how the robot reacts when it senses a black or white surface【4:3†source】 .

2. **Explanation using Course Context**: In your current implementation, your robot reads the sensor values to determine whether it's on black or white. When it detects white, it only turns right. You can modify this behavior by adding logic to rotate left as well. This will allow the robot to decide not just to turn in one direction but to choose either direction based on additional conditions (for example, more time spent on white).

3. **Connection to a Concrete Task**: You can implement the following changes in your code to allow for more flexible turning:

```cpp
#include <RobotMovingFunctions.h>
const int LIGHT_SENSOR_PIN = A0;
const int SURFACE_BRIGHTNESS_REFERENCE = 400; // threshold value

void setup()
{
  setIOpins();
  pinMode(LIGHT_SENSOR_PIN, INPUT);
}

void loop()
{
  int light_sensor_value = analogRead(LIGHT_SENSOR_PIN);

  if (light_sensor_value < SURFACE_BRIGHTNESS_REFERENCE)
  {
    // BLACK area, move left
    moveLeft();
    delay(100);
  }
  else
  {
    // WHITE area, randomly decide to turn left or right
    if (random(0, 2) == 0)  // Randomly choose to turn left or right
    {
      moveLeft();  // Turn left
    }
    else
    {
      moveRight(); // Turn right
    }
    delay(100);
  }
}
```

In the above code snippet, the robot will now randomly choose to turn either left or right when it detects white. This introduction of randomness will help prevent it from making 180-degree turns unnecessarily and allow it to continue searching for the black line in a more varied way.

